1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,949
Hello, and welcome to your Edexcel chemistry paper two revision lesson.

2
00:00:05,299 --> 00:00:13,157
We'll cover key concepts, groups in the periodic table, rates and energy changes, then fuels and the atmosphere.

3
00:00:13,507 --> 00:00:23,873
If your exam is one hour ten minutes, you take combined science. If it's one hour forty-five, you take separate chemistry, and there's an extra section at the end for you.

4
00:00:24,223 --> 00:00:29,469
Pause the video whenever you want, and try each question before I give the answer.

5
00:00:30,219 --> 00:00:38,542
Every atom has a tiny nucleus in the middle. It contains protons, with a charge of plus one, and neutrons, with no charge.

6
00:00:38,892 --> 00:00:43,917
Electrons, with a charge of minus one, move around the nucleus in shells.

7
00:00:44,267 --> 00:00:51,022
Protons and neutrons both have a mass of one. Electrons are so light, their mass is almost nothing.

8
00:00:51,372 --> 00:00:57,465
An atom has no overall charge, because it has the same number of protons and electrons.

9
00:00:58,215 --> 00:01:02,753
The atomic number, the smaller number, is the number of protons.

10
00:01:03,103 --> 00:01:07,675
The mass number, the bigger number, is protons plus neutrons.

11
00:01:08,025 --> 00:01:13,213
Sodium has atomic number eleven. So it has eleven protons and eleven electrons.

12
00:01:13,563 --> 00:01:18,867
Neutrons are mass number minus atomic number: twenty-three minus eleven is twelve.

13
00:01:19,217 --> 00:01:24,324
Isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons.

14
00:01:25,074 --> 00:01:30,796
Fill the shells two, then eight, then eight. Sodium has eleven electrons: two, eight, one.

15
00:01:31,146 --> 00:01:33,582
Chlorine has seventeen: two, eight, seven.

16
00:01:33,932 --> 00:01:39,062
The group number is the number of electrons in the outer shell. Sodium is in group one.

17
00:01:39,412 --> 00:01:42,707
The period, or row, is the number of shells.

18
00:01:43,057 --> 00:01:47,943
Metals are on the left of the periodic table. Non-metals are on the right.

19
00:01:48,293 --> 00:01:54,967
Mendeleev left gaps for elements not yet discovered, and predicted their properties. He was later proved right.

20
00:01:55,717 --> 00:02:00,870
An ion is a charged atom. Metals lose electrons to make positive ions.

21
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:07,034
Non-metals gain electrons to make negative ions. Both do this to get a full outer shell.

22
00:02:07,384 --> 00:02:15,057
Ionic bonding is between a metal and a non-metal. Electrons are transferred, and the opposite charges attract strongly.

23
00:02:15,407 --> 00:02:22,882
Ionic compounds have high melting points. They conduct electricity when molten or dissolved, because the ions can move.

24
00:02:23,232 --> 00:02:27,572
Covalent bonding is between non-metals. They share pairs of electrons.

25
00:02:27,922 --> 00:02:33,899
Small covalent molecules have low melting points, because the forces between molecules are weak.

26
00:02:34,649 --> 00:02:44,435
In a formula, the charges must cancel out. Magnesium is two plus and chloride is one minus, so we need two chlorides: MgCl₂.

27
00:02:44,785 --> 00:02:49,601
Equations must balance: the same number of each atom on both sides.

28
00:02:49,951 --> 00:02:54,674
There are two oxygens on the left, so put a two in front of MgO.

29
00:02:55,024 --> 00:03:00,131
Now there are two magnesiums on the right, so put a two in front of Mg. Balanced.

30
00:03:00,481 --> 00:03:04,113
Only ever change the big numbers in front, never the small ones.

31
00:03:04,863 --> 00:03:13,650
To find the relative formula mass, add up the atomic masses. Carbon dioxide is twelve plus sixteen plus sixteen: forty-four.

32
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,222
Calcium carbonate is forty plus twelve plus three sixteens: one hundred.

33
00:03:19,572 --> 00:03:24,412
Mass is always conserved. If the mass seems to go down, a gas escaped.

34
00:03:24,762 --> 00:03:34,048
Concentration: ten grams dissolved in two hundred and fifty centimetres cubed. First, change to decimetres cubed: zero point two five.

35
00:03:34,398 --> 00:03:39,400
Ten divided by zero point two five is forty grams per decimetre cubed.

36
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:47,555
Group one metals have one outer electron. They are soft, and stored under oil because they react with air and water.

37
00:03:47,905 --> 00:03:51,944
With water, they make a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.

38
00:03:52,294 --> 00:03:54,138
Lithium fizzes steadily.

39
00:03:54,488 --> 00:03:59,072
Sodium fizzes faster, melts into a ball, and whizzes around.

40
00:03:59,422 --> 00:04:02,984
Potassium reacts very fast, with a lilac flame.

41
00:04:03,734 --> 00:04:07,378
Reactivity increases going down group one. Here's why.

42
00:04:07,728 --> 00:04:11,372
Going down, the atoms get bigger, with more shells.

43
00:04:11,722 --> 00:04:15,029
So the outer electron is further from the nucleus,

44
00:04:15,379 --> 00:04:19,336
which means less attraction, so it is lost more easily.

45
00:04:21,086 --> 00:04:27,853
The halogens have seven outer electrons. They exist as pairs of atoms, called diatomic molecules.

46
00:04:28,203 --> 00:04:30,674
Chlorine is a pale yellow-green gas.

47
00:04:31,024 --> 00:04:33,506
Bromine is a red-brown liquid.

48
00:04:33,856 --> 00:04:37,999
Iodine is a dark grey solid, which gives a purple vapour.

49
00:04:38,349 --> 00:04:41,726
Going down, the melting and boiling points increase.

50
00:04:42,076 --> 00:04:47,403
The test for chlorine: damp blue litmus paper turns red, then is bleached white.

51
00:04:48,153 --> 00:04:50,949
Halogens react by gaining one electron.

52
00:04:51,299 --> 00:04:55,303
Going down the group, the outer shell is further from the nucleus.

53
00:04:55,653 --> 00:04:58,611
So it is harder to attract an extra electron.

54
00:04:58,961 --> 00:05:03,824
That's why reactivity decreases going down group seven. The opposite of group one!

55
00:05:04,574 --> 00:05:11,573
In a displacement reaction, a more reactive halogen pushes a less reactive one out of its salt solution.

56
00:05:11,923 --> 00:05:16,728
Chlorine plus potassium bromide gives potassium chloride plus bromine.

57
00:05:17,078 --> 00:05:19,421
Here is the balanced symbol equation.

58
00:05:19,771 --> 00:05:23,740
You see the solution turn orange, because bromine is made.

59
00:05:24,090 --> 00:05:30,926
Iodine plus potassium chloride: no reaction, because iodine is less reactive than chlorine.

60
00:05:31,276 --> 00:05:37,776
Halogens also react with metals to make salts called metal halides, like sodium chloride.

61
00:05:38,526 --> 00:05:43,040
The noble gases have a full outer shell, so they are very unreactive.

62
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:48,416
Helium is used in balloons, because it is less dense than air and does not burn.

63
00:05:48,766 --> 00:05:55,892
Argon is used in light bulbs and welding, because it is inert, so it stops metal reacting with oxygen.

64
00:05:56,242 --> 00:05:59,944
Going down the group, boiling point and density increase.

65
00:06:00,694 --> 00:06:04,640
Pause and try. Why is potassium more reactive than sodium?

66
00:06:10,990 --> 00:06:15,225
Answer: potassium's outer electron is further from the nucleus,

67
00:06:15,575 --> 00:06:19,370
so there is less attraction, and it is lost more easily.

68
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,694
The rate of reaction is how fast a reaction happens.

69
00:06:24,044 --> 00:06:32,773
Mean rate is the amount of product made, divided by the time. Sixty centimetres cubed of gas in thirty seconds is two centimetres cubed per second.

70
00:06:33,123 --> 00:06:36,453
For a reaction to happen, particles must collide,

71
00:06:36,803 --> 00:06:41,515
with enough energy. The minimum energy needed is the activation energy.

72
00:06:41,865 --> 00:06:48,132
To speed up a reaction, we need more frequent collisions, or more collisions with enough energy.

73
00:06:48,882 --> 00:06:56,230
Higher temperature: particles move faster, so there are more frequent collisions, and more of them have enough energy.

74
00:06:56,580 --> 00:07:02,406
Higher concentration: more particles in the same volume, so more frequent collisions.

75
00:07:02,756 --> 00:07:09,140
Higher pressure, for gases: the same particles in a smaller space, so more frequent collisions.

76
00:07:09,490 --> 00:07:16,152
Bigger surface area: a powder has more particles exposed than a lump, so more frequent collisions.

77
00:07:16,502 --> 00:07:23,652
A catalyst gives a route with a lower activation energy, and is not used up. Enzymes are biological catalysts.

78
00:07:24,402 --> 00:07:33,769
Edexcel uses two reactions. Marble chips with hydrochloric acid make carbon dioxide. Measure the volume of gas, or the mass lost.

79
00:07:34,119 --> 00:07:38,158
You can change the size of the chips, or the concentration of the acid.

80
00:07:38,508 --> 00:07:45,182
Sodium thiosulfate with hydrochloric acid goes cloudy. Time how long until the cross disappears.

81
00:07:45,532 --> 00:07:49,106
Here, you can change the temperature or the concentration.

82
00:07:49,456 --> 00:07:54,504
On the graph, steep means fast, and flat means the reaction has finished.

83
00:07:55,254 --> 00:07:59,676
An exothermic reaction gives out heat, so the temperature rises.

84
00:08:00,026 --> 00:08:05,631
Examples are burning, and neutralisation, which is an acid plus an alkali.

85
00:08:05,981 --> 00:08:10,519
An endothermic reaction takes in heat, so the temperature falls.

86
00:08:10,869 --> 00:08:16,173
Examples are thermal decomposition, and dissolving ammonium nitrate in water.

87
00:08:16,523 --> 00:08:23,324
In the practical, use a polystyrene cup with a lid. It is an insulator, so less heat is lost.

88
00:08:24,074 --> 00:08:27,091
Breaking bonds needs energy. That's endothermic.

89
00:08:27,441 --> 00:08:30,667
Making bonds releases energy. That's exothermic.

90
00:08:31,017 --> 00:08:34,951
Let's calculate the energy change for hydrogen plus chlorine.

91
00:08:35,301 --> 00:08:41,673
Breaking: four hundred and thirty-six, plus two hundred and forty-two, is six hundred and seventy-eight.

92
00:08:42,023 --> 00:08:48,163
Making two HCl bonds: two times four hundred and thirty-one is eight hundred and sixty-two.

93
00:08:48,513 --> 00:08:55,198
Break minus make is minus one hundred and eighty-four kilojoules per mole. It's negative, so it's exothermic.

94
00:08:55,948 --> 00:09:03,342
On a reaction profile, an exothermic reaction has the products lower than the reactants. Energy was given out.

95
00:09:03,692 --> 00:09:07,080
In an endothermic reaction, the products are higher.

96
00:09:07,430 --> 00:09:12,246
The hump, from the reactants up to the top, is the activation energy.

97
00:09:12,596 --> 00:09:15,009
A catalyst makes the hump lower.

98
00:09:15,759 --> 00:09:20,123
A hydrocarbon is a compound of hydrogen and carbon only.

99
00:09:20,473 --> 00:09:25,556
Crude oil is a complex mixture of hydrocarbons, mostly alkanes.

100
00:09:25,906 --> 00:09:30,072
It is finite. It is being used up much faster than it forms.

101
00:09:30,422 --> 00:09:37,027
It gives us fuels, and feedstock, which means raw materials for making plastics and other chemicals.

102
00:09:37,777 --> 00:09:42,570
In fractional distillation, the oil is heated until it evaporates.

103
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:47,840
The gas rises up a column that is hot at the bottom and cool at the top.

104
00:09:48,190 --> 00:09:53,239
Each fraction condenses when it reaches its boiling point, and is collected.

105
00:09:53,589 --> 00:09:57,662
Petrol is used in cars. Kerosene is fuel for aircraft.

106
00:09:58,012 --> 00:10:03,061
Diesel oil is for cars and trains. Fuel oil is for ships and power stations.

107
00:10:03,411 --> 00:10:08,238
Gases are for heating and cooking, and bitumen surfaces roads and roofs.

108
00:10:08,988 --> 00:10:14,246
Bigger molecules have higher boiling points, are thicker, and are harder to set alight.

109
00:10:14,596 --> 00:10:19,134
The alkanes are a homologous series. They have the same general formula,

110
00:10:19,484 --> 00:10:22,628
each one differs from the next by CH₂,

111
00:10:22,978 --> 00:10:25,530
and they have similar chemical properties.

112
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:30,093
The first four are methane, ethane, propane and butane.

113
00:10:30,843 --> 00:10:36,518
Complete combustion, with plenty of oxygen, makes carbon dioxide and water.

114
00:10:36,868 --> 00:10:42,671
Incomplete combustion, without enough oxygen, also makes carbon monoxide and soot.

115
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:49,080
Carbon monoxide is toxic. It stops your blood carrying oxygen. You can't see it or smell it.

116
00:10:49,430 --> 00:10:55,872
Sulfur impurities in fuels burn to make sulfur dioxide, which causes acid rain.

117
00:10:56,222 --> 00:11:03,882
In hot engines, nitrogen and oxygen from the air react to make nitrogen oxides. These cause acid rain too.

118
00:11:04,632 --> 00:11:09,031
Hydrogen is a clean fuel. When it burns, the only product is water.

119
00:11:09,381 --> 00:11:12,235
But it's a gas, so it's hard to store.

120
00:11:12,585 --> 00:11:14,046
It's explosive,

121
00:11:14,396 --> 00:11:18,144
and making it often uses energy from fossil fuels.

122
00:11:18,894 --> 00:11:23,989
Crude oil has too many long chains, and not enough short ones like petrol.

123
00:11:24,339 --> 00:11:29,062
Cracking breaks large alkanes into smaller, more useful molecules.

124
00:11:29,412 --> 00:11:32,615
The vapour is heated and passed over a hot catalyst.

125
00:11:32,965 --> 00:11:37,061
For example, decane cracks into octane and ethene.

126
00:11:37,411 --> 00:11:42,843
Cracking makes alkenes, which have a carbon to carbon double bond. They're used to make plastics.

127
00:11:43,593 --> 00:11:51,799
The early atmosphere came from volcanoes. It was mostly carbon dioxide, with water vapour, and little or no oxygen.

128
00:11:52,149 --> 00:11:56,095
As the Earth cooled, water vapour condensed to form the oceans.

129
00:11:56,445 --> 00:12:03,061
Carbon dioxide went down. It dissolved in the oceans, and was locked in rocks and fossil fuels.

130
00:12:03,411 --> 00:12:08,285
Oxygen went up because of photosynthesis by algae and plants.

131
00:12:08,635 --> 00:12:11,524
The test for oxygen: a glowing splint relights.

132
00:12:11,874 --> 00:12:17,538
Today, the air is about seventy-eight percent nitrogen and twenty-one percent oxygen.

133
00:12:18,288 --> 00:12:22,988
The greenhouse gases are carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour.

134
00:12:23,338 --> 00:12:27,226
Short wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere.

135
00:12:27,576 --> 00:12:31,324
The warm Earth gives off longer wavelength infrared radiation.

136
00:12:31,674 --> 00:12:36,687
Greenhouse gases absorb this and send some back, keeping the Earth warm.

137
00:12:37,037 --> 00:12:42,817
Humans add more by burning fossil fuels, cutting down forests, and farming cattle.

138
00:12:43,567 --> 00:12:49,196
Evidence shows carbon dioxide levels and global temperatures have both risen together.

139
00:12:49,546 --> 00:12:54,630
But older data is less accurate. There were fewer measurements, from fewer places.

140
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:59,076
Effects of climate change include rising sea levels and flooding,

141
00:12:59,426 --> 00:13:02,605
more extreme weather, and problems growing food.

142
00:13:02,955 --> 00:13:08,015
We can reduce it with renewable energy, carbon capture, and using less energy.

143
00:13:08,765 --> 00:13:13,802
This section is only for separate chemistry. Flame tests identify metal ions.

144
00:13:14,152 --> 00:13:21,929
With sodium hydroxide, copper ions give a blue solid, iron two gives green, and iron three gives brown.

145
00:13:22,279 --> 00:13:27,351
Sulfate ions: add barium chloride, and a white solid forms.

146
00:13:27,701 --> 00:13:34,572
Halide ions: add silver nitrate. Chloride gives white, bromide cream, and iodide yellow.

147
00:13:34,922 --> 00:13:39,007
The test for alkenes: bromine water turns from orange to colourless.

148
00:13:39,757 --> 00:13:44,654
Let's recap. Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus. Electrons are in shells.

149
00:13:45,004 --> 00:13:49,821
Group one gets more reactive going down. Group seven gets less reactive.

150
00:13:50,171 --> 00:13:53,989
Rates depend on frequent collisions, with enough energy.

151
00:13:54,339 --> 00:13:58,365
Exothermic gives heat out. Endothermic takes heat in.

152
00:13:58,715 --> 00:14:04,751
Cracking makes alkenes. And the atmosphere went from volcanoes, to oceans, to oxygen.

153
00:14:05,101 --> 00:14:08,013
Well done for revising. Good luck in your mock!
